  "body": "Part of #361. Follows #370 (milling bodies align by their ISO 13399 coordinate systems).\n\n**What happens.** A STEP for a milling tool that names ISO 13399 coordinate systems places itself (`step_csys::align_tool_body` gives the axis and tip offset). A lathe holder does not: the tool editor's lathe placement and MCP `set_lathe_tool_body` always need the radial direction, the spindle direction and the tip point by hand, even when the file names its coordinate systems.\n\n**Open question to settle first (from public sources only, as #370 did):** which named systems of a turning tool item give the cutting reference point and the two directions, and how the hand of the tool (left / right / neutral) maps onto the lathe's working side. Cite the public previews in the decision file; if the names cannot be confirmed, stop and say so.\n\n**Fix.** A reader that turns the file's systems into a `LathePlacement` (radial, along, tip), offered by the editor on import (\"aligned by ISO 13399 coordinate systems\") and by `set_lathe_tool_body` when no placement is given; ambiguous or absent systems fall back by name, as the milling path does.\n\n**Done means:** a synthetic STEP (our own) naming the systems places a lathe holder with no numbers typed; one without them says why and keeps the manual placement. Pinned in `tool_csys` style tests.\n",
